

Zinux-Linux

The hardware compatibility layer of Zinux.

Zinux-Linux is a Linux fork maintained as part of the Zinux project.

Its purpose is simple:

Do not throw away decades of working hardware support.

Linux contains an enormous amount of accumulated knowledge about real
hardware: device drivers, hardware quirks, protocols, workarounds,
firmware interfaces and years of practical engineering.

Zinux intends to stand on those shoulders.

⸻

Why does this exist?

Zinux explores a different approach to operating-system hardware
support:

Can a local AI construct a hardware-specific driver when it is
needed, rather than requiring every possible driver to be permanently
maintained in the operating system?

That is an ambitious research question.

It would be wasteful to make Zinux answer that question by first
reimplementing decades of existing hardware support.

Zinux-Linux therefore provides a bridge to the existing world.

                         ZINUX
                           │
              ┌────────────┴────────────┐
              │                         │
       Existing hardware          New hardware
              │                         │
       Zinux-Linux drivers        AI-generated
              │                    native drivers
              │                         │
              └────────────┬────────────┘
                           │
                      Zinux system

The goal is coexistence, not replacement for its own sake.

⸻

What is Zinux-Linux?

Zinux-Linux is primarily concerned with:

* existing Linux drivers
* hardware compatibility
* established driver interfaces
* firmware support
* device discovery
* hardware-specific workarounds
* compatibility infrastructure required by Zinux

Code should remain in the language and architecture appropriate to the
upstream Linux subsystem.

C is fine.

Rust is fine.

Assembly is fine.

Other existing technologies are fine when they are appropriate.

This repository is not an attempt to rewrite Linux in Zig.

⸻

Relationship with Zinux

The repositories have deliberately different purposes.

Zinux-Linux

The past and present.

It provides access to existing hardware support and preserves the
practical knowledge accumulated by the Linux ecosystem.

Zinux

The experiment and the future.

Zinux is primarily written in Zig and investigates:

* capability-based hardware access
* isolated drivers
* local AI
* Driver Plans
* driver validation
* sandboxed execution
* dynamic device support
* AI-generated hardware-specific logic

The two projects can therefore coexist.

Zinux-Linux
    │
    │ existing drivers
    ▼
┌──────────────────────┐
│                      │
│       Zinux          │
│                      │
│  kernel              │
│  capabilities        │
│  IPC                 │
│  isolation           │
│  native Zig drivers  │
│  AI driver synthesis │
│                      │
└──────────────────────┘

⸻

Not a competition with Linux

Zinux-Linux exists precisely because Linux has already solved an
enormous part of the hardware problem.

The project does not claim that Linux should be discarded.

Quite the opposite.

Linux is one of the foundations on which Zinux can experiment.

If an existing Linux driver works reliably, there is little reason to
immediately replace it.

The interesting question begins where existing approaches become
expensive to maintain, difficult to support, or unavailable for new
hardware.

⸻

Drivers as accumulated knowledge

A Linux driver is more than source code.

It often represents years of accumulated knowledge about:

* undocumented hardware behavior
* register ordering
* timing requirements
* race conditions
* firmware problems
* hardware revisions
* vendor-specific quirks
* failure recovery

Zinux-Linux therefore also serves as a potential source of knowledge
for future Zinux research.

One long-term research direction is to investigate whether existing
drivers can be analyzed to extract information useful for AI-assisted
Driver Plan generation.

Linux driver
     │
     ├── resource usage
     ├── register operations
     ├── state transitions
     ├── interrupts
     ├── DMA
     └── hardware quirks
              │
              ▼
        Driver knowledge
              │
              ▼
          Zinux AI

This does not mean that Linux driver source code is automatically a
formal specification.

It is evidence.

⸻

Compatibility before reinvention

The project follows a simple rule:

Use existing solutions when they are good enough. Reinvent them when
there is a reason.

A device does not become more interesting merely because Zinux can
rewrite its driver.

The real research value lies in determining where dynamically
constructed drivers provide an advantage.

⸻

Long-term direction

The ideal future is not necessarily:

Linux drivers → zero

It may instead look like:

                    Hardware
                       │
          ┌────────────┴────────────┐
          │                         │
   Existing driver             New device
          │                         │
     Linux/C/etc.                  AI
          │                         │
          │                    Driver Plan
          │                         │
          │                    Native Zig
          │                         │
          └────────────┬────────────┘
                       │
                  Zinux kernel
                       │
              capabilities / IPC
                       │
                    hardware

Some drivers may remain permanently maintained.

Others may eventually be generated on demand.

The system does not need to decide that question in advance.

⸻

A bridge, not a destination

Zinux-Linux exists so that Zinux can move forward without pretending
that the last decades of operating-system engineering never happened.

The ambition of Zinux is not to erase that history.

It is to build on it.

Stand on Linux’s shoulders. Build something different from there.

⸻

